nough to move the race out of Likely Republican.", "watch": "Whether follow-up surveys from UNH and Saint Anselm confirm the tightening seen in the mid-September UNH poll, or the gap reverts toward double digits.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Kelly Ayotte (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kelly Ayotte, elected in 2024, is seeking a second term in a state that has tilted slightly Democratic in federal races while repeatedly electing Republican governors. Democrats nominated former Executive Councilor Cinde Warmington, who is running an affordability-focused campaign and has drawn endorsements from national figures including Eric Holder.\n\nPolling since the September primary shows Ayotte ahead in every survey, ranging from four points in the latest University of New Hampshire poll to eleven points for the New York Times/Siena. Earlier fall surveys showed leads near ten points, so the race may be narrowing, but that rests on a single poll. Ratings mostly place the contest in Likely Republican territory, and prediction markets give Warmington roughly a four percent chance. The forecast expects Ayotte to win by around seven to eight points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0651, "margin": -8.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0566, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0654, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0669, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0672, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0698, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0784, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1135, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0916, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0954, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0873, "margin": -7.5}]}